    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65332",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9",
      "knowledge_point": "Describing Properties of the Secant Function",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- $x=-\\pi $ is not in the domain of the function. $$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is in the range of the function. $$\n- $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is defined for all real numbers except the values $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n,$ thus it is defined for $x=-\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is not in the range.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function $y=sec⁡x$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, thus $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote.",
      "answer_fields": [
