    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-103691",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function",
      "problem": "If $sec⁡x=3\\sqrt{3},$ then what is $sec⁡(x+8\\pi )?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=3\\sqrt{3},$ then $sec⁡(x+8\\pi )=3\\sqrt{3}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "3\\sqrt{3}"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-3\\sqrt{3}"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\sqrt{3}-3"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "3+\\sqrt{3}"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "3\\sqrt{3}"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-103691",
        "kp_title": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function",
        "kp_href": null
      }
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-66603",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function",
      "problem": "If $sec⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then what is $4sec⁡(x+10\\pi )-3?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 5\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then $sec⁡(x+10\\pi )=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4sec⁡(x+10\\pi )-3 & =4(\\frac{5}{2})-3 \\\\  & =2(5)-3 \\\\  & =10-3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
