    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-103692",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function",
      "problem": "If $sec⁡x=\\sqrt{7},$ then what is $sec⁡(x-16\\pi )?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-8$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot (-8)\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x-16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=\\sqrt{7},$ then $sec⁡(x-16\\pi )=\\sqrt{7}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
