      "knowledge_point_id": "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function",
      "problem": "If $sec⁡x=2\\sqrt{5},$ then what is $\\sqrt{5}sec⁡(x+20\\pi )-3?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=10$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 10\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+20\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=2\\sqrt{5},$ then $sec⁡(x+20\\pi )=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}sec⁡(x+20\\pi )-3 & =\\sqrt{5}(2\\sqrt{5})-3 \\\\  & =2(5)-3 \\\\  & =10-3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
