    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-136623",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d4b378b0-30e5-59a8-b9f7-882b5e226a2e",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Secant Function",
      "problem": "If $sec⁡x=-2,$ then $5sec⁡(-x+12\\pi )+1=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x+12\\pi ) & =sec⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 6) \\\\  & =sec⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x) & =sec⁡x \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5sec⁡(-x+12\\pi )+1 & =5\\cdot (-2)+1 \\\\  & =-10+1 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
