    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-11006",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9",
      "knowledge_point": "Describing Properties of the Secant Function",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements regarding the graph of $y=\\sec x$ are true?\n\n- The function takes the value $1$ at $x = 2n\\pi,$ where $n$ is an integer\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty,+\\infty)$\n\n- The function has zeros at $x = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2 \\pi,\\dots$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's have a look at the graph of $y=\\sec x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- The secant takes the value $1$ at $x =  2 n\\pi .$ Therefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The range of $f(x) = \\sec x$ is $(-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, \\infty)$. Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- The secant graph does not intersect the $x$-axis. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-11007",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function",
      "problem": "If $\\sec x =2 \\sqrt 2$ then what is $\\sec(x+8\\pi)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y = \\sec x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = \\sec{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x & =sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =sec⁡(x+8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\sec{x} = 2\\sqrt 2,$ then $\\sec{(x+8\\pi)} = 2\\sqrt 2.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-11014",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d4b378b0-30e5-59a8-b9f7-882b5e226a2e",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Secant Function",
      "problem": "If $\\sec{x} = -\\dfrac{11}{6},$ then $\\sec(-x - 14\\pi) =$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y = \\sec{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\sec{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\sec{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sec(-x) = \\sec{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\sec{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x-14\\pi ) & =sec⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =sec⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\sec{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x) & =sec⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sec(-x - 14\\pi) = -\\dfrac{11}{6}.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
  ]
